Make the following focused fixes to the Log Streamer. Do not change existing functionality or the current log row structure.

1. HEADER
- Remove the duplicate "Log Streamer" heading above the controls.
- Keep the existing "Log Stream" tab/header as the identifier.
- Put Namespace, Tier, Type, and their dropdown selectors on the same single horizontal line.
- Keep the Start/Stop button and Auto-scroll on the same line where space permits.
- Make the header compact and avoid unnecessary vertical space.

2. POD COLOR IDENTIFICATION
- Assign exactly ONE stable color to each unique pod.
- Every log entry belonging to the same pod must use the same color.
- Do NOT generate a different color for each log line.
- Use a thin vertical colored bar on the LEFT side of each log entry/heading as the pod identifier.
- Keep the actual log text, timestamp, and pod information unchanged.
- Colors must remain stable when new logs arrive or older logs are loaded.

3. OLDER LOG LOADING
- Initial load must display only 100 log entries.
- Do NOT continuously load older logs automatically.
- When the user scrolls to/reaches the top, load exactly 50 older entries.
- Show a small "Loading older logs..." indicator while those 50 entries are being fetched.
- Once loaded, hide the indicator and stop loading.
- Only load the next 50 when the user reaches the top again.
- Preserve the user's current scroll position when older logs are prepended. Do not jump the viewport.
- Prevent multiple simultaneous older-log requests.

4. PERFORMANCE
- Do not allow uncontrolled/infinite loading of historical logs.
- Add a configurable maximum number of log entries kept in frontend memory to prevent the browser from becoming slow.
- Do not change the backend log source or existing streaming architecture unless required to fix this behavior.

5. AUTO-SCROLL
- If the user is at the bottom/live position, continue automatically following new logs.
- If the user scrolls away from the bottom, stop automatically moving the viewport.
- New logs may continue arriving, but must not force the user back to the bottom.
- Existing Auto-scroll functionality should remain available.
- Do not automatically scroll the user while older logs are being loaded.

6. START/STOP/RESTART
- Ensure Start creates only one active stream/background task.
- Stop must cleanly stop the stream and any associated background task.
- Restart must not leave the previous connection/task running.
- Prevent duplicate log entries caused by multiple active streams.

7. LOG STREAM TAB
- Ensure the "Log Stream" tab/header text remains clearly visible.
- It must not become faded, gray, or otherwise unreadable after the stream runs for some time.
- Preserve the existing active/inactive visual behavior, but ensure sufficient contrast.

IMPORTANT:
- Keep the current two-row log entry structure exactly as it is.
- Do NOT convert timestamp and pod information into separate columns.
- Do NOT redesign the log viewer.
- Do NOT add Kibana-style features yet.
- Make only the above fixes.
- After implementation, validate that initial loading, live streaming, scrolling upward, loading 50 older entries, auto-scroll, Stop, and Restart all behave correctly.
